# Aquinas's reply distinguishes "self-evident in itself" from "self-evident to us." Within the objection, though, "self-evident" in P1 and C is the same thing: known as soon as the terms are known. So I find no shift there.
# The weak point of the argument is P5 together with A2. P5 moves from "the word is understood" to "God exists mentally," meaning God is present in the intellect as something conceived. A2 then compares that kind of existence with actual existence. That is a joining of different claims, not one claim read in two senses. "Mental" and "actual" are already kept apart as separate claims, and each is used consistently.
